<|channel>s_thought
<channel|>We often celebrate public library apps like Libby or Hoopla as free magic, but there is a hidden maintenance cost that taxpayers eventually shoulder. These platforms aren't truly "free"; libraries pay significant licensing fees and per-circulated transaction costs to digital wholesalers. As digital reading grows, these budget lines swell, often competing with the funds needed for physical books and in-person programming. We enjoy the convenience of an infinite digital shelf, but it comes with a mounting subscription bill behind the scenes. [end of text]


